---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 The Viv authors
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Toc from '../components/Toc.astro';
import programs from '../data/programs.json';
import { atlasHref } from '../lib/atlasLink.js';
import { codeSpans } from '../lib/richText.js';

const { groups, programs: all } = programs;
const totalLines = all.reduce((s, p) => s + p.lines, 0);
const totalDefs = all.reduce((s, p) => s + p.defs, 0);
// programs without test blocks whose result a test in lang/viv/tests checks from outside
const RUST_TESTED = new Set(['fem_poisson.viv', 'scf_sto3g.viv', 'geomopt.viv', 'geomopt_water.viv', 'md_polymer.viv', 'h2_sto3g.viv']);
const TOC: [string, string][] = [
  ['quantum-chemistry', 'Quantum chemistry'],
  ['plasma', 'Plasma physics'],
  ['nuclear-astro', 'Nuclear & astro'],
  ['numerics', 'Numerical methods'],
  ['differentiation', 'Autodiff'],
  ['symbolic', 'Symbolic'],
  ['language', 'Language features'],
  ['basis', 'The basis-set ladder'],
  ['convergence', 'A convergence-rate check'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Example programs · Viv"
  description={`All ${all.length} Viv programs in the repo, from Hartree–Fock and plasma kinetics to neutron-star structure, finite elements, autodiff and symbolic regression, with the reference each checked program is compared against.`}
  eyebrow="Example programs"
  heading="Programs and the references they are checked against">
  <div class="withtoc">
    <div>
  <p class="lede">
    {all.length} programs, {totalLines.toLocaleString()} lines, {totalDefs} definitions. Beside each result is the
    reference to compare it with: a published energy, an independent implementation, or a convergence rate set by
    theory. A program with tests checks its result itself, and a few are checked by the compiler's own tests; for
    the rest, marked <em>no test</em>, the comparison is the reader's.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every program here is loaded into the <a href="/workbench">workbench</a> and the
    <a href="/cli">terminal</a>, so you can run, compile, hash and diff any of them without
    installing anything. In the repo they live in
    <code>lang/viv/examples/</code>.
  </p>

  {
    groups.map((g) => {
      const items = all.filter((p) => p.group === g.slug);
      return (
        <section>
          <h2 id={g.slug}>{g.title}</h2>
          <p>{g.blurb}</p>
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Program</th>
                  <th>What it computes</th>
                  <th>Result</th>
                  <th class="r">Lines</th>
                  <th />
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {items.map((p) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      <a href={`/programs/examples/${p.file}`}><code>{p.file}</code></a>
                      <a class="ref atlas" href={atlasHref(`examples/${p.file}`)}>atlas</a>
                    </td>
                    <td class="blurb" set:html={codeSpans(p.blurb)} />
                    <td>
                      {p.result ? (
                        <>
                          <span class="res">{p.result}</span>
                          <span class="ref">{p.reference}</span>
                          {p.tests === 0 && (
                            <span class="ref">{RUST_TESTED.has(p.file) ? 'checked by a test in lang/viv/tests' : 'no test'}</span>
                          )}
                        </>
                      ) : (
                        <span class="ref">—</span>
                      )}
                    </td>
                    <td class="r num">{p.lines}</td>
                    <td class="marks">
                      {p.tests > 0 && <span class="pill pill-ok">{p.tests} test{p.tests === 1 ? '' : 's'}</span>}
                      {p.plots > 0 && <span class="pill">{p.plots} fig</span>}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>
      );
    })
  }

  <h2 id="basis">The basis-set ladder for water</h2>
  <p>
    At one fixed water geometry, the variational principle requires each larger basis to lower the
    Hartree–Fock energy toward the basis-set limit, and it does:
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th class="r">Functions</th><th class="r">E(SCF) / Eₕ</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>STO-3G (s, p)</td><td class="r num">7</td><td class="r num">−74.9421</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6-31G (split valence)</td><td class="r num">13</td><td class="r num">−75.9356</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6-31G* (+ d on O)</td><td class="r num">19</td><td class="r num">−76.0192</td></tr>
        <tr><td>the HF basis-set limit</td><td class="r num">—</td><td class="r num">≈ −76.067</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The STO-3G energy the program prints, −74.94208, is within about a microhartree of the value in
    T. D. Crawford's published programming projects (−74.9420787); no test checks it. On top of the
    converged reference, <code>scf_sto3g.viv</code> computes the MP2 correlation energy through four
    O(N⁵) quarter-transforms: it prints −0.0491496 Eₕ for water, against the reference −0.049149636. Helium and
    neon give exactly zero, as they should, because a minimal basis leaves them no virtual orbitals.
  </p>

  <h2 id="convergence">A convergence-rate check</h2>
  <p>
    <code>fem_poisson.viv</code> solves −∇²u = f on the unit square with P1 triangles against a
    manufactured solution. P1 elements are second-order accurate, so the error has to
    <em>quarter</em> each time the mesh halves. Measured ratios as the mesh refines: 3.45, 3.74,
    3.87, approaching 4. The check needs only the rate, not a table of reference values.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The program runs one mesh and prints its <code>rms</code> error. The test
    <code>lang/viv/tests/fem_poisson.rs</code> recompiles it at N = 8, 16, 32… and checks the
    ratios, so <code>cargo test</code> checks the rate, not the program's own output.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>fem_poisson_records.viv</code> is the same solver rewritten with records — a
    <code>Mesh</code> value and a per-element <code>Tri</code> instead of nine loose scalar
    arguments. It produces identical numbers. Passing a record does not copy its array fields, so passing a
    mesh around copies no array data.
  </p>

  <div class="nextprev">
    <span><a href="/workbench">← Run one in the workbench</a></span>
    <span><a href="/install">Compile one natively →</a></span>
  </div>
</div>
    <Toc items={TOC} />
  </div>

  <style>
    h2 {
      scroll-margin-top: 72px;
    }
    table {
      font-size: 0.83rem;
    }
    .blurb {
      color: var(--text-muted);
      max-width: 36ch;
    }
    .res {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      color: var(--data-text);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .ref {
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-faint);
    }
    .r {
      text-align: right;
      padding-right: 1.2rem;
    }
    .marks {
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .marks .pill {
      margin: 0 0 2px 3px;
      font-size: 11px;
    }
    section p {
      color: var(--text-muted);
      font-size: 0.93rem;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
